---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import Plate from '../components/Plate.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

// LAUNCH GATE (spec §8.1 / open item #2): Geordie's section must not go
// live until the employment-lawyer review of his employment agreement
// clears. Every specific in his bio is drawn from the verified sources in
// the spec; do not add credentials he does not hold. Per REBUILD SPEC v4
// §5.4, the former employer is not named anywhere on the site.
// OPEN ITEM #8: Jesse's bio is a draft pending his confirmation of what is
// public. Concrete achievements to be added by him.
---

<Article
  title="Who We Are — The People on Your Project"
  heading="The people on your project"
  kicker="A small practice by design"
  description={`The principals of ${SITE.name} and the independent specialists retained on every engagement. No account managers, no handoffs — the people you meet are the people doing the work.`}
>
  <p>
    A small practice by design. No account managers, no handoffs, no junior staff sent to site meetings. The
    people you meet are the people doing the work.
  </p>

  <Plate
    src="/plates/plate-10-forest.svg"
    alt="Layered coastal forest ridgelines in fog"
    caption="Based on Salt Spring Island. At work wherever the project is."
    class="my-12 aspect-[21/9]"
  />

  <h2>Geordie Flanagan</h2>
  <p>Geordie has sat on both sides of a residential build.</p>
  <p>
    He began as a client. He and his wife Jess set out to build on a beautiful and difficult lot on Salt
    Spring Island, with a young daughter and a second on the way and very little time. He hired a builder,
    went through the entire process, and learned what it costs to build on a site that fights you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then he joined the company. Over five years inside a prefab housing company he moved from Operations
    Manager to head of sales to Vice President and Partner, working on design-led residential projects across
    the Pacific Northwest. He has spoken publicly about how construction pricing is actually assembled — the
    difference between what a client is quoted and what a house costs to deliver.
  </p>
  <p>
    He knows how builders quote, how they schedule, how site work and production fit together, and which
    clauses in a construction agreement have room in them. He knows because he used to write them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before construction he founded GEORGE Collective, a brand and partnerships firm, and spent three years at
    AntiSocial. He lives on Salt Spring Island.
  </p>

  <h2>Jesse</h2>
  <p>
    Jesse runs the commercial and client-facing side of the practice: structuring engagements, and holding
    decisions documented and moving. He holds permanent residency in both Canada and the United States and
    can contract on either side of the border — which matters for families holding property on both sides of
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>The specialists we engage</h2>
  <p>We are not engineers, and we don’t pretend to be.</p>
  <p>
    We engage independent specialists — structural engineering, cost consulting, site inspection — and name
    them in the proposal for your project, matched to its region and scope. Construction counsel on referral
    where a contract or a dispute requires it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each is engaged by us and paid by us on your behalf. None has a relationship with your contractor. We
    would rather bring in the right specialist than offer a generalist’s opinion on a stamped drawing.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a small firm</h2>
  <p>
    One of us came from inside the industry; the other builds the process, the reporting and the
    communication discipline. Most failures in this work are communication failures rather than technical
    ones — a decision made twice, a change never priced, a report nobody read.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/project-review">Request a project review</a>, or write to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
